## 0. What changed in v2

The face-anchor set was supplied on the issue (character sheet v2.0 + face/body
reference sheet). That closed the Persona Bible §7 reference-pack handoff, so
the render gate opened and assets were produced.

v1 shipped written creative only and paused visuals because §7 requires an
approved anchor. v2 adds **11 delivered visual assets** with machine-verified
disclosure QA.

**Honest scope note — read this before approving.** No image-generation
provider is reachable from this runtime (no image model credential, no local
generation service; the only configured model provider is a text LLM). So
frames were **not** synthesised. Each asset is a **deterministic composition
built from the approved anchor sheet itself**: a registered head panel from the
locked reference, plus typography. Two consequences, both deliberate:

1. **Identity is exact.** Every frame contains a crop of the approved
   reference. Face drift is structurally impossible — there is no generative
   step that could drift.
2. **The photographic content is reference-grade, not a new photograph.** These
   are correctly composed, correctly labelled, correctly proportioned
   production **masters/deliverable layouts**: they are what each post ships as
   its cover/frame with the required disclosure burned in. They are *not*
   freshly imagined scene photography (a new park path, a new kitchen counter).

What that means for the two asset classes:

| Class | Delivered now | Still needs a generator |
|---|---|---|
| Static (Fri) + carousel (Tue, 5 slides) | **Production-ready.** These are the final still compositions. | nothing |
| Reels (Mon/Wed/Thu/Sat/Sun) | **Cover + shot-card masters**: the frame that carries the script, shot timings and the disclosure. | per-shot b-roll frames for the 15–20 s cuts |

So: the disclosure, identity, modesty, category mix and written creative are
complete and verified for all seven posts. The gap is scene b-roll for the five
reels, which needs a generation provider or a shoot. I have not labelled the
reel covers as if they were the full motion asset.

